\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of your flow test \expnd0\expndtw-2 results:\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb8\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1256\ri362\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 CFM measurements from "sharp edged" orifices are not accurate at low flow pressures (Flow Pres).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For example, for a flow manometer calibrated in "Flow %", an eighth inch distance at low flows may be the difference between 20 and 25% Flow.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, at\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the high end of the scale, an eighth of an inch is the difference between 98 and 98.5% Flow.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 An eighth inch reading error at the low end may result in 5/20 = 25% error.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However at the high end this same reading error would only be .5/98 = 0.5% error.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 When possible, select a flow range to keep the Flow Pres at the high end of the manometer \expnd0\expndtw-2 scale.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1256\ri532\qj\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Since the Leak CFM is usually small, the Flow Pres may be very low on the Flow (inclined) manometer.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Therefore, the Leak CFM measured may not be accurate, and may actually contribute more to an error than neglecting it.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For example, the leakage is actually 2 CFM, but you measure it incorrectly as 5 CFM.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Using 5 as Leak CFM will cause more error in the final CFM calculation than using 0 for Leak CFM.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 To accurately measure Leak CFM, first minimize all leaks for all your flow tests, but continue to measure Leak CFM.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 A Leak CFM whi